PROPOSAL ONLY — NOT APPLIED, NOT RUN (lib/prisma.ts is outside lane L1's scope).

SF-136 defence in depth. Cycle 2 closes the start-timeout path at the owning boundaries: every application transaction
is opened by withTenantContext / withRlsBypass / checkDbRateLimit / reserveTx, and none of them lets Prisma abandon a
start any more (maxWait out of reach; the pool's own 10 s deadline bounds the wait) — pinned by
tests/unit/sec-l1-store-tx-bound ("no code outside the three owned boundaries opens a Prisma transaction") and proved
by tests/integration/sec-l1-start-timeout-db (both roles). The library defect itself remains: a future caller that
passes a short maxWait straight to prisma.$transaction would reopen it. This patch makes the pool refuse to keep ANY
client that is released while the server says it is inside a transaction (ReadyForQuery status 'T' or 'E'), whoever
released it: the client is ended, so the server rolls the transaction back, and pg-pool drops it (pg-pool 3.13
_release() emits 'release' and then removes a client whose _ending is set). It relies on two pg internals
(client.connection's 'readyForQuery' message and client._ending), so it needs a unit test against the pinned pg
version before it is applied.

--- a/lib/prisma.ts
+++ b/lib/prisma.ts
@@
 import { PrismaClient } from "@prisma/client";
 import { PrismaPg } from "@prisma/adapter-pg";
+import pg from "pg";
